          "distinct": true,
          "id": "6962e64655667e3e005e493762b4a4886ed3bbe7",
          "message": "perf(velvet): skip the patch of a memoized row that rendered nothing (#1493)\n\n## What & why\n\nBuilds on #1491 (merge that first; this branch contains its commits).\n\nIn the keyed-list benchmark, an operation touching one or two of 1,000 memoized rows cost ~30 ms. The general path's `CommitLeaf` re-patched every memoized row that bailed: the bail handed back the very `ElementNode` it had committed, and `PatchNode` still ran the post-children class passes and a nested child reconcile for the row and each of its children. The indexed and keyed paths already skip identical instances; the general path did not, so a memo bail did not skip its subtree patch as `React.memo` does.\n\n- `ComponentRegistry.GetOrCreateInline` reports whether it reused a fiber that rendered nothing; `CommitLeaf` skips `PatchNode` for that owner's identical `ElementNode` unless the element is a z-layer placeholder or carries stacking facts, a fiber below it is dirty, or the walk is inside Suspense or AnimatePresence.\n- `FiberElementCleaner.RemoveCleaned` tries the remembered index and the one below before `IndexOf`, so clearing N rows is no longer N²/2 compares.\n- `ComponentFiber` keeps a tail pointer, so appending N inline children is no longer N(N-1)/2 chain hops.\n\nBehaviour: a non-memoized component below an element of a bailed memoized row is no longer re-rendered by the parent's re-render, which is what `React.memo` does.\n\nDocumentation: no doc impact; the memoization guides already describe this behaviour. CHANGELOG fragment under `Changelog~/unreleased/`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Regression tests: `BailedRowSubtreeTests`, `ElementRemovalIndexLookupTests`, `ComponentFiberChildTailTests` (count work, not wall-clock)\n- [x] CHANGELOG fragment\n- [x] No doc impact\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01FoK7YxdRjMGmfmRkEEmS88)_",
